                <pb facs="0105" n="71" sameAs="frus:frus1958-60v12/pg71"/>

                <head>20. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>On July 14 General Abdul Karim Qassim led a military coup which overthrew the
                    government of King Faisal of Iraq. The King, the Crown Prince, other members of
                    the royal family, and Prime Minister Nuri Said were killed. The new provisional
                    Government of Iraq announced that it was leaving the Arab Union. Documentation
                    on the Iraqi coup and the United States response is printed in the compilation
                    on Iraq.</p>
                <p>On July 15 the Eisenhower administration sent 3,500 troops into Lebanon in
                    response to a request from President Chamoun for U.S. and British intervention
                    in Lebanon. Documentation on this decision is in <ref
                        target="frus:frus1958-60v11/pg_107">volume XI, pages 107</ref> ff.</p>
                <p>On July 17 the British Government airlifted two battalions into Jordan at the
                    request of the Jordanian Government. For the U.S. role in that decision, see
                        <ref target="frus:frus1958-60v11/pg_264"><hi rendition="#italic">ibid</hi>.,
                        pages 264</ref> ff.</p>
            </div>
